From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 09:06:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] module-droid-fm: play the FM tuner through a direct audio HAL
 patch

Android plays FM radio without copying a sample: AudioFlinger asks the
audio HAL for a device-to-device patch, AUDIO_DEVICE_IN_FM_TUNER -> the
output device, and the codec routes the tuner straight to the DAC.
Opening the FM input as a source and looping it back, which is what
UBports, Volla and Sailfish do, records the built-in microphone on a
MediaTek MT6739 (the HAL opens input_device 0x80000004, not 0x80002000),
and switching the source's port to the tuner with a capture open aborts
PulseAudio.

module-droid-fm makes that patch on the HAL module-droid-card already has
open, and stands for the radio inside PulseAudio as an ordinary playback
stream, by default on the "fm" sink:

 - The stream plays silence, so its sink stays running and the HAL never
   puts the playback path to standby under the radio.
 - The active port of the droid sink the stream ends up on (through any
   filter sinks) is the patch's output device; a port change or a move
   re-creates the patch. A combined port such as
   output-speaker+wired_headphone, which alerts switch to, keeps the
   patch on the wired device, so a notification does not cut the radio.
 - The stream's volume and mute, and those of its sink, become the HAL's
   FM volume, so the policy's class volume, the master volume and a
   policy duck all apply. Corked, muted or silent, the patch is released.

The MT6739 HAL takes the FM volume as the gain of the patch's sink port
(set_audio_port_config), not from set_parameters, and reads gain.values[0]
as an unchecked index into its 16-step table, not as millibels: on
headphones -64 dB, then -42..0 dB in 3 dB steps. The stream's volume is
matched to that table in dB (step_db=, default 3; max_step=, default 15),
so the radio moves 4 dB per volume key like any other stream and a -20 dB
duck is heard as one. The patch also needs a primary output stream open,
or the HAL fails it with -ENOSYS; the droid primary sink keeps one.

The tuner itself (power, frequency) is left to the FM service, which
loads this module while the radio is on.

HALs without the patch API get mode=parameters. The Qualcomm msm8953
HAL of an Android 9 vendor image returns -ENOSYS from
create_audio_patch and set_audio_port_config; it starts FM with
set_parameters("handle_fm=<output device | AUDIO_DEVICE_OUT_FM>"), stops
it with "handle_fm=<output device>" and takes "fm_volume=<0..1>" as a
linear gain. In that mode the module sends those instead, the stream's
audible volume as the linear fm_volume, and leaves everything else as in
patch mode. patch stays the default.

Tested on a MediaTek MT6739 (Android 11 HAL) with the tuner on 95.0 MHz
and wired headphones: the HAL gets createAudioPatch device
0x80002000->0x00000008 and the radio is heard; the volume keys step it
from silent to full; an alert switches the sink to speaker+headphones
without releasing the patch, and with audiod giving alerts priority over
FM, ducks the radio from step 6 to 1 and back. Again on the MT6739 with
mode= added, patch mode, on the speaker: raising the volume from
audiod's 0 creates 0x80002000->0x00000002 and steps the HAL's fm_volume
0.063 -> 0.355 -> 1.0; mute and volume 0 release the patch, unmute
creates it again; switching the port to the earpiece releases it and
asks for 0x80002000->0x00000001, which this HAL refuses, and switching
back to the speaker creates it again.

Tested on a Xiaomi Mi A1 (msm8953) with mode=parameters and wired
headphones: the HAL gets handle_fm=1048584 (0x100008), applies its
play-fm path and sets fm_volume=0.3945; indoors, 95.0 MHz is heard
through noise, and the volume keys change fm_volume.

Built warning-free in its own lines with GCC 15 and Clang for armv7,
aarch64 and x86-64; the only warnings are version.h's #warning about the
audio device API version.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
